  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9069',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Uninstall</em> at the bottom of the page. Verify the list of modules to be uninstalled and configuration to be deleted on the confirmation page, and click <em>Uninstall</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9070',
  'source' => 'Wait for the module to be uninstalled. You should be returned to the <em>Uninstall</em> page with a message saying the module was uninstalled.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9071',
  'source' => 'Set up your site to respond appropriately to site errors, including 403 and 404 page responses.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9072',
  'source' => 'What are 403 and 404 responses?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9073',
  'source' => 'When a user visits a web page, the web server sends a response code in addition to the page content. A normal, non-error response has code 200. If the page does not exist on the site, the response code is 404. If the page exists, but the user is not authorized to visit the page, the response code is 403. The core software provides default responses for both 403 and 404 codes, but if you prefer, you can create your own pages for each.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9074',
  'source' => 'What other errors can occur?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9075',
  'source' => 'Under some situations, your site can generate error messages. These can be due to user errors (such as entering invalid values in a form, or incorrect configuration), PHP runtime errors, or software bugs. Some errors may result in a <em>white screen of death</em> (a totally blank web page response); less drastic errors will generate error messages. You can configure what happens when an error message is generated.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9076',
  'source' => 'If desired, create pages to use for 403 and 404 responses. Note the URLs for these pages.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9077',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Error pages</em> section, enter the URL for your 403/404 pages, starting after the site home page URL. For example, if your site URL is <em>https://example.com</em> and your 404 page is <em>https://example.com/not-found</em>, you would enter <em>/not-found</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9078',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Development</em> &gt; <em>@log_settings_link</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9079',
  'source' => 'For a production site, select <em>None</em> under <em>Error messages to display</em>. For a site that is in development, select one of the other options, so that you are more aware of the errors the site is generating.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9080',
  'source' => 'For each of these themes, if the theme is in the <em>Uninstalled themes</em> section, click the <em>Install</em> link to install the theme. Wait for the theme to be installed (translations might be downloaded). You should be returned to the <em>Appearance</em> page.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9081',
  'source' => 'Locate the theme that you want to be your default theme, which should now be in the <em>Installed themes</em> section. If it is not already labeled as the <em>default theme</em>, click the <em>Set as default</em> link.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9082',
  'source' => 'Performance',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9083',
  'source' => 'Clear the data in the site cache.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9084',
  'source' => 'What is the cache?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9085',
  'source' => "Some of the calculations that are done when your site loads a page take a long time to run. To save time when these calculations would need to be done again, their results can be <em>cached</em> in your site's database. There are internal mechanisms to <em>clear</em> cached data when the conditions or assumptions that went into the calculation have changed, but you can also clear cached data manually. When your site is misbehaving, a good first step is to clear the cache and see if the problem goes away.",
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9086',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Development</em> &gt; <em>@performance_link</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9087',
  'source' => "Click <em>Clear all caches</em>. Your site's cached data will be cleared.",
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9088',
  'source' => '<a href="https://www.drupal.org/docs/user_guide/en/prevent-cache.html">Concept: Cache (Drupal User Guide)</a>',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9089',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>@people_link</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9090',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Add user</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9091',
  'source' => 'Enter the <em>Email address</em>, <em>Username</em>, and <em>Password</em> (twice) for the new user.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9092',
  'source' => 'Verify that the <em>Roles</em> checked for the new user are correct.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9093',
  'source' => 'If you want the new user to receive an email message notifying them of the new account, check <em>Notify user of new account</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9094',
  'source' => 'Optionally, change other settings on the form.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9095',
  'source' => 'You will be left on the <em>Add user</em> page; repeat these steps if you have more user accounts to create.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9096',
  'source' => 'Configure settings related to how user accounts are created and deleted.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9097',
  'source' => 'What are the settings related to user account creation and deletion?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9098',
  'source' => 'You can make it possible for new users to register themselves for accounts, with or without email verification or administrative approval. Or, you can make it so only administrators with <em>Administer users</em> permission can register new users.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9099',
  'source' => 'You can configure what happens to content that a user created, if their account is <em>canceled</em> (deleted).',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9100',
  'source' => 'What are variables in email message text?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9101',
  'source' => '<em>Variables</em> are short text strings, enclosed in square brackets [], that you can insert into configured email message text. When an individual message is generated, data from your site is substituted for the variables. Some commonly-used variables are:',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9102',
  'source' => '[site:name]: The name of your website.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9103',
  'source' => '[site:url]: The URL of your website.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9104',
  'source' => '[site:login-url]: The URL where users can log in to your site.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9105',
  'source' => "[user:display-name]: The user's displayed name.",
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9106',
  'source' => "[user:mail]: The user's email alias.",
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9107',
  'source' => '[user:one-time-login-url]: An expiring URL that a user can use to log in once, if they need to reset their password.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9108',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>People</em> &gt; <em>@account_settings_link</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9109',
  'source' => 'Select the method you want to use for creating user accounts, and check or uncheck the box that requires email verification, to match the settings you want for your site.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9110',
  'source' => 'Select the desired option for what happens to content that a user created if their account is canceled.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9111',
  'source' => 'Optionally, edit the text of email messages related to user accounts.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9112',
  'source' => 'Verify that the other settings are correct.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9113',
  'source' => 'What is a user?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9114',
  'source' => 'A user is anyone accessing or viewing your site. <em>Anonymous</em> users are users who are not logged in, and <em>Authenticated</em> users are users who are logged in.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9115',
  'source' => 'What is a role?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9116',
  'source' => '<em>Roles</em> are used to group and classify users; each user can be assigned one or more roles. There are also special roles for all anonymous and all authenticated users.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9117',
  'source' => 'What is a permission?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9118',
  'source' => 'Granting a <em>permission</em> to a role allows users who have been assigned that role to perform an action on the site, such as viewing content, editing or creating  a particular type of content, administering settings for a particular module, or using a particular function of the site (such as search).',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9119',
  'source' => 'Overview of managing user accounts and visitors',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9120',
  'source' => 'The core User module allows users to register, log in, and log out, and administrators to manage user roles and permissions. The core Ban module allows administrators to ban certain IP addresses from accessing the site. Depending on which modules you have installed on your site, the related topics below will help you with tasks related to managing user accounts and visitors.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9121',
  'source' => 'Modify the permissions for an existing role.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9122',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>People</em> &gt; <em>@permissions_link</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9123',
  'source' => 'Review the permissions for the role, paying particular attention to the permissions marked with <em>Warning: Give to trusted roles only; this permission has security implications.</em> Uncheck permissions that this role should not have, in the row of the permission and the column of the role; check permissions that this role should have.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9124',
  'source' => 'Create a new role.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9125',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>People</em> &gt; <em>@roles_link</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9126',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Add role</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9127',
  'source' => 'Enter the desired <em>Role name</em>. If desired, click <em>Edit</em> to change the <em>Machine name</em> for the role.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9128',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Save</em>. You should be returned to the <em>Roles</em> page and your new role should be in the role list.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9129',
  'source' => 'Update or delete an existing user account.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9130',
  'source' => 'Enter all or part of the user name or email address of the user account you want to update or delete, and click <em>Filter</em>. A short list of user accounts, including the account of interest, should be shown in the table; if not, modify the filter text until you can find the account of interest.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9131',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Edit</em> in the <em>Operations</em> area of the account of interest.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9132',
  'source' => "To delete the user account, scroll to the bottom and click <em>Cancel account</em>. Select what you want to happen to the user's content on the next screen, and click <em>Cancel account</em>.",
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9133',
  'source' => 'To update the user account, enter new values in the form and click <em>Save</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9134',
  'source' => 'On some sites, new content and content revisions need to be <em>moderated</em>. That is, they need to pass through several <em>states</em> before becoming visible to site visitors. The collection of states and the definition of the transitions between states is known as a <em>workflow</em>. For example, new content might start out in a <em>Draft</em> state, and then might need to pass through several <em>Review</em> states before it becomes <em>Published</em> on the live site.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9135',
  'source' => "The core Content Moderation module allows you to expand on core software's \"unpublished\" and \"published\" states for content. It allows you to have a published version that is live, but have a separate working copy that is undergoing review before it is published. This is achieved by using workflows to apply different states and transitions to entities as needed.",
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9136',
  'source' => 'The core Workflows module allows you to manage workflows with states and transitions.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9137',
  'source' => 'See the related topics listed below for specific tasks and background information.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9138',
  'source' => 'View official announcements related to Drupal',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9139',
  'source' => 'What are Drupal announcements?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9140',
  'source' => 'A feed of announcements about the Drupal project and Drupal Association programs.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9141',
  'source' => 'The purpose of this feed is to provide a channel for outreach directly to Drupal site owners. This content must be highly relevant to site owners interests, serve the strategic goals of the project, and/or promote the sustainability of the project and the Drupal Association.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9142',
  'source' => 'The module sources its content from a JSON feed generated from <a href="https://www.drupal.org/about/announcements">here</a>. The governance policy for the content is documented <a href="https://www.drupal.org/node/3274085">here</a>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9143',
  'source' => 'How can I see the Announcements in my site?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9144',
  'source' => 'If you have the toolbar module enabled, you will see a direct link to them in the toolbar. If the toolbar module is not enabled, the content can always be accessed in the <em>@actions_link</em> page.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9145',
  'source' => 'Who can see the Announcements?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9146',
  'source' => 'Users with the <em>@permissions_link</em> permission can view Drupal announcements.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9147',
  'source' => 'Announcement module overview',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9148',
  'source' => 'Users with <em>workflows permissions</em> (typically administrators) can configure workflows. See Permissions &gt; <em>@workflows_permissions_link</em> to configure workflows permissions.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9149',
  'source' => 'Decide which workflow states you need; for example, <em>Concept</em>, <em>Review</em>, and <em>Final</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9150',
  'source' => '<em>Label</em>: the state name',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9151',
  'source' => '<em>Published</em>: if checked, when content reaches this state it will be made visible on the site (to users with permission).',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9152',
  'source' => '<em>Default revision</em>: if checked, when content reaches this state it will become the default revision of the content; published content is automatically the default revision.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9153',
  'source' => 'Decide which state content should be created in.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9154',
  'source' => 'Decide on the list of allowed transitions between states. For example, you might want a transition between <em>Concept</em> and <em>Review</em>. Each transition has a label; for example, the Concept to Review transition might be labeled "Review concept".',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9155',
  'source' => 'Decide which roles should have permissions to make each transition; see @user_overview_topic for an overview of roles and permissions.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9156',
  'source' => 'Decide which <em>entity types</em> and subtypes the workflow should apply to. Only entity types that support revisions are possible to define workflows for. See @content_structure_topic for more information on content entities and fields.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9157',
  'source' => 'To implement your plan, in the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Workflow</em> &gt; <em>@workflows_link</em>. A list of workflows is shown, including the default workflow <em>Editorial</em> that you can adapt.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9158',
  'source' => 'Enter a name in the <em>Label</em> field, select <em>Content moderation</em> from the <em>Workflow type</em> field, and click <em>Save</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9159',
  'source' => 'Verify that the <em>States</em> list matches your planned states. You can add missing states by clicking <em>Add a new state</em>. You can edit or delete states by clicking <em>Edit</em> or <em>Delete</em> under <em>Operations</em> (if the <em>Delete</em> option is not available, you will first need to delete any <em>Transitions</em> to or from this state).',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9160',
  'source' => 'Verify that the <em>Transitions</em> list matches your plan. You can add missing transitions by clicking <em>Add a new transition</em>. You can edit or delete transitions by clicking <em>Edit</em> or <em>Delete</em> under <em>Operations</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9161',
  'source' => 'Under <em>This workflow applies to:</em>, find the entity type that you want this workflow to apply to, such as Content revisions, Content block revisions, or Taxonomy term revisions. Click <em>Select</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9162',
  'source' => 'Check the entity subtypes that you want to apply the workflow to. For example, you might choose to apply your workflow to the <em>Page</em> content type, but not to <em>Article</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9163',
  'source' => 'Under <em>Workflow settings</em>, select the <em>Default moderation state</em> for new content.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9164',
  'source' => 'Follow the steps in @user_permissions_topic to assign permissions for each transition to roles. The permissions are listed under the <em>@content_moderation_permissions_link</em> section; there is one permission for each transition in each workflow.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9165',
  'source' => 'Optionally (recommended), create a view for your custom workflow, to provide a page for content editors to see what content needs to be moderated. You can do this if the Views UI module is installed, by following the steps in the related <em>Creating a new view</em> topic listed below under <em>Related topics</em>. When creating the view, under <em>View settings</em> &gt; <em>Show</em>, select the revision data type you configured the workflow for, and be sure to display the <em>Workflow State</em> field in your view.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9166',
  'source' => 'Change the workflow state of a particular entity. See @workflows_overview_topic for an overview of workflows, and @content_structure_topic for an overview of content entities.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9167',
  'source' => 'Who can change workflow states?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9168',
  'source' => 'Users with <em>content moderation permissions</em> can change workflow states. There are separate permissions for each transition. See Permissions &gt; <em>@content_moderation_permissions_link</em> to configure content moderation permissions.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9169',
  'source' => 'Find the entity that you want to moderate in either the content moderation view page, if you created one, or the appropriate administrative page for managing that type of entity (such as the administration page for content items; see @content_link).',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9170',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Edit</em> to edit the entity.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9171',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Structure</em> &gt; @layout_link.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9172',
  'source' => 'What are content blocks?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9173',
  'source' => 'Content blocks are blocks whose content you can edit. You can define one or more <em>block types</em>, and attach fields to each block type. Content blocks can be placed just like blocks provided by other modules.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9174',
  'source' => 'Overview for managing blocks',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9175',
  'source' => 'The <em>Block</em> module allows you to place blocks in regions of your installed themes, and configure block settings. The <em>Block Content</em> module allows you to manage block types and content blocks. See the related topics listed below for specific tasks.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9176',
  'source' => '<a href="https://www.drupal.org/docs/user_guide/en/blocks-chapter.html">Blocks (Drupal User Guide)</a>',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9177',
  'source' => 'Configure text formats on the site.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9178',
  'source' => 'What are text filters and text formats?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9179',
  'source' => 'A text filter is a processing step that can be applied to text, either to transform it in some way (such as converting URLs in the text into HTML links), or to defend against potentially-dangerous input from site users. A text format is an ordered sequence of text filters. Text filters are provided by modules; text formats are managed by the core Filter module.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9180',
  'source' => 'Text fields that have "formatted" in the field type name, such as <em>Text (formatted)</em>, use text formats. Users choose the text format when editing the field text; when the field text is shown on the site, it is processed by the chosen text format. Administrators can configure text formats and assign permissions for who can use each format. If the core Text Editor module is enabled, administrators can also associate visual editors with text formats.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9181',
  'source' => 'What text filters are available?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9182',
  'source' => 'Some of the more commonly used text filters are:',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9183',
  'source' => 'Limits which HTML tags can be used; useful both for site security and enforcing site design.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9184',
  'source' => 'Line breaks in HTML source are displayed as horizontal spaces. This filter converts line breaks into HTML paragraph and line break tags.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9185',
  'source' => 'Takes plain URLs in text and turns them into HTML links.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9186',
  'source' => 'For text formats that allow HTML image tags, restricts images to URLs within this site.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9187',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Configure</em> to configure an existing text format, or <em>+ Add text format</em> to create a new text format.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9188',
  'source' => 'Enter the desired <em>Name</em> for the text format.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9189',
  'source' => 'Check the <em>Roles</em> that can use this text format. Some HTML tags allow users to embed malicious links or scripts in text. To ensure security, anonymous and untrusted users should only have access to text formats that restrict them to either plain text or a safe set of HTML tags. <strong>Improper text format configuration is a security risk.</strong>',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9190',
  'source' => 'If the core Text Editor module is installed, see the related topic to connect a text editor to this text format.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9191',
  'source' => 'Under <em>Enabled filters</em>, check the text filters that you want to use.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9192',
  'source' => 'Under <em>Filter processing order</em>, drag the filters to the correct order. Choose the order carefully; for example, if you have a filter that results in a particular HTML tag being added to the text, that should run after a filter that restricts HTML tags, to avoid deleting the new tags the first filter added.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9193',
  'source' => 'Under <em>Filter settings</em>, verify and adjust the settings for each active filter that has configuration options.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9194',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Save configuration</em>, which will return you to the <em>Text formats and editors</em> page.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9195',
  'source' => 'Repeat these steps if you have additional text formats to configure.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9196',
  'source' => 'Define a block type and its fields.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9197',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Structure</em> &gt; <em>@types_link</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9198',
  'source' => 'Click  <em>Add block type</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9199',
  'source' => 'Optionally, click <em>Manage form display</em> or <em>Manage display</em> to change the editing form or field display for your block type.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9200',
  'source' => 'Create a content block, which can later be placed on the site.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9201',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Content</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9202',
  'source' => 'Open the @library_link tab.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9203',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Add content block</em>. If you have more than one block type defined on your site, click the name of the type you want to create.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9204',
  'source' => 'Media  queries are a formal way to encode breakpoints. For instance, a width breakpoint at 40em would be written as the media query "(min-width: 40em)". Breakpoints are really just media queries with some additional meta-data, such as a name and multiplier information.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9205',
  'source' => 'Managing breakpoints and breakpoint groups overview',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9206',
  'source' => 'Working with breakpoints in Drupal',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9207',
  'source' => 'A language detection method is a way for your site to decide what language should be used to display text. This decision is made by evaluating a series of detection methods for languages; the first detection method that gets a result will determine which language is used. If you have more than one language on your site, you can configure the detection methods that are used and the order they are evaluated, for both user interface text and content text. Configuration text always uses the interface text method, and most sites use the same detection methods for all types of text.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9208',
  'source' => 'Use the language from the URL, which can either be in a path prefix like <em>example.com/fr/page_path</em> for French (fr) language, or in a domain like <em>fr.example.com/page_path</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9209',
  'source' => 'For logged in users, use the language they have set in their profiles.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9210',
  'source' => 'Use the language most recently selected from a <em>Language switcher</em> block.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9211',
  'source' => 'Use the language from a session parameter or a request parameter; for example, adding <em>?language=fr</em> to the end of the URL.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9212',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Region and language</em> &gt; <em>Languages</em> &gt; @detection_link.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9213',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Configure</em> on each enabled detection method and verify that its configuration is correct (or update the configuration if not).',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9214',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Region and language</em> &gt; @languages_link.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9215',
  'source' => 'If your language is in the <em>Language name</em> list, select it and click <em>Add language</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9216',
  'source' => 'If your language is not in the list, select <em>Custom language...</em> and enter the <em>Language code</em>, <em>Language name</em>, and <em>Direction</em> for the language. Click <em>Add custom language</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9217',
  'source' => 'Wait for the translation system to be updated. Also, if you have the core Update Manager module installed and translations are available for your language, wait for translations to be downloaded. You should be returned to the <em>Languages</em> page when this completes.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9218',
  'source' => 'Translate user interface text strings from English into a non-English language that is configured on your site.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9219',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Region and language</em> &gt; @translate_link.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9220',
  'source' => 'Using the filters, search for a string or set of strings that you want to translate; make sure to select the correct <em>Translation language</em> if you have more than one non-English language on your site.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9221',
  'source' => 'Repeat these steps until all of the desired user interface text is translated for all languages on your site.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9222',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Region and language</em> &gt; @language_link.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9223',
  'source' => 'Look at the <em>Interface translation</em> column in the language table, to find the percentage of user interface text that has been translated for each language.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9224',
  'source' => 'Optionally, click <em>Check manually</em> to update the report.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9225',
  'source' => 'View the report to find out if any languages have translation updates that you can download.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9226',
  'source' => 'Import a file (.po extension) containing translations for user interface text.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9227',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Region and language</em> &gt; <em>User interface translation</em> &gt; @import_link.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9228',
  'source' => 'Browse to find the <em>Translation file</em> you want to import. Select the language and check the desired import options.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9229',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Import</em> and wait for your file to be imported.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9230',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Region and language</em> &gt; <em>@config_translation_link</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9231',
  'source' => 'Enter translations for the translatable text fields for the configuration item, and save.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9232',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Region and language</em> &gt; <em>@translation_settings_link</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9233',
  'source' => 'Under <em>Custom language settings</em>, find the content entity types that should have customized language settings on your site. Check the box next to each one. A section will appear below the list with settings for that entity type.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9234',
  'source' => 'For each entity type you checked, in the settings section below check the boxes for each entity sub-type that should be <em>Translatable</em> on your site. If the entity type does not have sub-types, there is just one check box for the entity type as a whole.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9235',
  'source' => 'For each entity type or subtype, select the <em>Default language</em>. Also, if you want to have languages other than the default available when you create content, check <em>Show language selector on create and edit pages</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9236',
  'source' => 'For each <em>Translatable</em> type or sub-type, look through the list of fields for the type or sub-type, and verify that only the fields that should be translatable are checked. For example, you would probably want to translate a <em>Title</em> field, but you might not want to translate a <em>Start date</em> field.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9237',
  'source' => '<em>Contextual links</em> give users with the <em>Use contextual links</em> permission quick access to administrative tasks related to areas of non-administrative pages. For example, if a page on your site displays a block, the block would have a contextual link that would allow users with permission to configure the block. If the block contains a menu or a view, it would also have a contextual link for editing the menu links or the view. Clicking a contextual link takes you to the related administrative page directly, without needing to navigate through the administrative menu system.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9238',
  'source' => 'Make sure that the core Contextual Links module is installed, and that you have a role with the <em>Use contextual links</em> permission. Optionally, make sure that a toolbar module is installed (either the core Toolbar module or a contributed module replacement).',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9239',
  'source' => 'Make the contextual links button visible by hovering your mouse over that area in the page. In most themes, this button looks like a pencil and is placed in the upper right corner of the page area (upper left for right-to-left languages), and hovering will also temporarily outline the affected area. Alternatively, click the contextual links toggle button on the right end of the toolbar (left end for right-to-left languages), which will make all contextual link buttons on the page visible until it is clicked again.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9240',
  'source' => 'While the contextual links button for the area of interest is visible, click the button to display the list of links for that area. Click a link in the list to visit the corresponding administrative page.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9241',
  'source' => 'Add a field to an entity sub-type; see @content_structure_topic for an overview of entity types and sub-types, as well as an overview of field types.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9242',
  'source' => 'Find the particular sub-type that you want to add the field to, and click <em>Manage fields</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9243',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Add field</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9244',
  'source' => 'In <em>Add a new field</em>, select the type of field you want to add; see @content_structure_topic for an overview of field types.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9245',
  'source' => 'The <em>Label</em> field should now be visible; enter a label for the field, which is used as the field label for both content editing and content display.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9246',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Save and continue</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9247',
  'source' => 'On the next screen, enter a value for <em>Allowed number of values</em>. You can limit the field to one value per entity item, a set number of values, or set it to have unlimited values. Click <em>Save field settings</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9248',
  'source' => 'On the next screen, optionally edit the settings for the field, which vary depending on what field type you are creating. For all fields, you can edit the <em>Label</em>, <em>Help text</em> (text to be displayed below the field on the content editing page), and <em>Required field</em> (to make it so a value must be entered in order to save the content when editing). You can also configure a default value for the field.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9249',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Save settings</em>. You should be returned to the <em>Manage fields</em> page, with your new field in the list.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9250',
  'source' => 'Configure the <em>widgets</em> used to edit the fields of an entity sub-type, their order on the form, and the widget settings. See @content_structure_topic for background information.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9251',
  'source' => 'Find the particular sub-type that you want to configure the editing form for, and click <em>Manage form display</em> in the <em>Operations</em> list.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9252',
  'source' => 'Drag any fields that you do not wish to see on the editing form to the <em>Disabled</em> section.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9253',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Widget</em> column, select the widget for editing each field.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9254',
  'source' => 'After selecting the desired widgets, click the settings gear in each row to change the settings for the widget.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9255',
  'source' => 'Test the editing form for your entity sub-type by editing or creating an entity. If needed, return to these steps to further refine the form.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9256',
  'source' => 'Configure the <em>formatters</em> used to display the fields of an entity sub-type, their order in the display, and the formatter settings. See @content_structure_topic for background information.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9257',
  'source' => 'Find the particular sub-type that you want to configure the display of, and click <em>Manage display</em> in the <em>Operations</em> list.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9258',
  'source' => 'Drag any fields that you do not wish to see in the display to the <em>Disabled</em> section.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9259',
  'source' => "In the <em>Label</em> column, select the position for each field label in the display, or <em>- Hidden -</em> to hide a label. You can also choose <em>- Visually Hidden-</em> if you want the label's text to appear in the HTML page, so that screen readers and search engines can read it, but it will not be visible.",
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9260',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Format</em> column, select the formatter for displaying each field.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9261',
  'source' => 'After selecting the desired formatters, click the settings gear in each row to change the settings for the formatter.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9262',
  'source' => 'Test the display for your entity sub-type by viewing an entity. If needed, return to these steps to further refine the display.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9263',
  'source' => 'In <em>Add a new field</em>, select the type of reference field you want to add. The <em>Reference</em> section of the select list shows the most common types of reference field; choose <em>Other...</em> if the entity type you want to reference is not listed.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9264',
  'source' => 'On the next screen, verify that the type of entity you want to reference is shown in <em>Type of item to reference</em>, or select it if not. Enter a value for <em>Allowed number of values</em>. You can limit the field to one value per entity item, a set number of values, or set it to have unlimited values. Click <em>Save field settings</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9265',
  'source' => 'On the next screen, optionally edit the settings for <em>Label</em>, <em>Help text</em> (text to be displayed below the field on the content editing page), and <em>Required field</em> (to make it so a value must be entered in order to save the content when editing).',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9266',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Reference type</em> section, you will usually want to limit the entity sub-types that can be referenced; for example, if you are creating a <em>Content</em> reference, you can check one or two <em>Content type</em> choices. The choices will be easier for content editors to scan if you also choose a sort value (normally the entity title or label field).',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9267',
  'source' => 'Users with the <em>@content_permissions_link</em> permission (typically administrators) can create new content types.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9268',
  'source' => 'In the <em>Name</em> field, enter a name for the content type, which is how it will be listed in the administrative interface.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9269',
  'source' => 'Optionally, enter a <em>Description</em> for the content type. You may also want to adjust some of the settings in the vertical tabs section of the edit page.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9270',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Save and manage fields</em>. Your content type will be created, and assuming you have the core Field UI module installed, you will be taken to the <em>Manage fields</em> page for the content type. (If you do not have the core Field UI module installed, the button will say <em>Save</em> instead.)',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9271',
  'source' => 'If you have the core Field UI module installed, follow the steps in the related topics to add fields to the new content type, set up the editing form, and configure the display.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9272',
  'source' => 'Adding a Content Type (Drupal User Guide)',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9273',
  'source' => 'Setting Up Content Structure (Drupal User Guide)',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9274',
  'source' => 'A <em>content item</em> is a type of content entity for page-level content, which can have fields that store text, HTML markup, images, attached files, and other data. See @content_structure_topic for more about content entities and fields.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9275',
  'source' => 'Overview of managing content',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9276',
  'source' => 'Access the Content overview page',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9277',
  'source' => 'Find a content item and edit it, or update a group of content items in bulk. See @node_overview_topic for more about content types and content items.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9278',
  'source' => '<em>Title</em>: key word(s) from the title',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9279',
  'source' => '<em>Language</em>',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9280',
  'source' => 'Basic Page Management (Drupal User Guide)',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9281',
  'source' => 'Editing a Content Item (Drupal User Guide)',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9282',
  'source' => 'Users with the <em>@content_permissions_link</em> permission can visit the <em>Content</em> page as described in this topic. Each content type has its own create permissions. For example, to create content of type Article, a user would need the Article: Create new content permission. In addition, users with the <em>Bypass content access control</em> or <em>Administer content</em> permission can create content items of all types. Some contributed modules change the permission structure for creating content.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9283',
  'source' => 'On the content edit form, enter the <em>Title</em> of your content, which will show as the page title when the content item is displayed on a page, and also as the label for the content item in administration screens.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9284',
  'source' => 'Leave the <em>Published</em> field checked to publish the content immediately, or uncheck it to make it unpublished. Unpublished content is generally not shown to non-administrative site users.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9285',
  'source' => 'Optionally, click <em>Preview</em> to preview the content.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9286',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Save</em>. You will see the content displayed on a page.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9287',
  'source' => 'Creating a Content Item (Drupal User Guide)',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9288',
  'source' => 'What content visits are tracked?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9289',
  'source' => 'The core History module tracks when each logged-in user has most recently visited each content item page on the site. This allows content to be marked as <em>new</em> or <em>updated</em> for each user, meaning that it was newly created or has been updated since the last time they visited its page. These records are kept for one month, meaning that content older than one month is never marked as new or updated.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9290',
  'source' => 'You can display the new/updated status of content by creating or editing a view. There is a <em>Has new content</em> field for <em>Content</em> views, which displays the new/updated marker. There is also a <em>Has new content</em> filter, which limits the view to new and updated content.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9291',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Add image style</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9292',
  'source' => 'Enter a descriptive <em>Image style name</em>, and click <em>Create new style</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9293',
  'source' => 'Under <em>Effect</em>, choose an effect to apply and click <em>Add</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9294',
  'source' => 'Configure the effect on the next page. Most effects require some additional configuration after they are added.  For example, for the <em>Crop</em> effect, enter the <em>Width</em> and <em>Height</em> to crop the image to, and choose the <em>Anchor</em> point. Click <em>Add effect</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9295',
  'source' => 'The image style can now be used to format a field containing an image in your layouts or traditional field displays. It can also be used as part of a responsive image style. See related topics below for more information.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9296',
  'source' => 'What are the parts of a layout?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9297',
  'source' => 'Verify your layout. You can check <em>Show content preview</em> to show a preview of what your layout will look like, or uncheck it to see the names of the fields and blocks in each section.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9298',
  'source' => 'Building Layouts Using the Layout Builder UI',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9299',
  'source' => 'If there is not already a media type for the type of media you want to use on your site, click <em>Add media type</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9300',
  'source' => 'Enter a <em>Name</em> and <em>Description</em> for your media type, and select the <em>Media source</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9301',
  'source' => 'For most media sources, there is additional information that will need to be stored with your media item, in a field on your media type. Under <em>Media source configuration</em>, select an existing field to re-use to store this information, or select <em> - Create -</em> to create a new field.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9302',
  'source' => 'Note the types of metadata in the <em>Field mapping</em> section that can be mapped to fields on your media type.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9303',
  'source' => 'Optionally, add additional fields for the metadata noted above or for other information that you want to store to your media type by clicking on <em>Manage fields</em> (see related topic below).',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9304',
  'source' => 'If you have added metadata fields, click <em>Edit</em>. Under <em>Field mapping</em>, select the fields you added for each piece of metadata information.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9305',
  'source' => 'You can now use this media type by adding a Media reference field to any content entity sub-type. See related topic below.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9306',
  'source' => 'What is a view?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9307',
  'source' => 'A <em>view</em> is a listing of items on your site; for example, a block showing the most recent comments, a page listing news items, or a list of registered users. The listings can be formatted in a table, grid, list, calendar, RSS feed, and other formats (some output formats may require you to install additional contributed modules).',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9308',
  'source' => 'What are the components of a view?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9309',
  'source' => 'When you first create a view, you will specify what type of <em>base data</em> is being displayed in the view, which cannot be changed. After choosing a base data type, you can edit the following components, which allow you to specify which data to output, in what order, and in what format:',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9310',
  'source' => '<em>Displays</em>: whether the output goes to a page, block, feed, etc.; a single view can have multiple displays, each with different settings.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9311',
  'source' => '<em>Format</em>: the output style for each display, such as content item, grid, table, or HTML list.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9312',
  'source' => '<em>Fields</em>: if the Format allows, the particular fields to display.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9313',
  'source' => '<em>Filter criteria</em>: criteria to limit the data to output, such as whether the content is published, the type of content, etc. Filters can be <em>exposed</em> to let users choose how to filter the data.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9314',
  'source' => '<em>Sort criteria</em>: how to order the data. Sorting can also be exposed to users.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9315',
  'source' => '<em>Header</em> and <em>Footer</em>: content to display at the top or bottom of the view display.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9316',
  'source' => '<em>No results behavior</em>: what to do if the filter criteria result in having no data to display.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9317',
  'source' => '<em>Pager</em>: how many items to display, and how to paginate if there are additional items to display.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9318',
  'source' => '<em>Advanced</em> &gt; <em>Contextual filters</em>: like regular filters, except the criteria come from the <em>context</em>, such as the current date, page the view is displayed on, etc.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9319',
  'source' => '<em>Advanced</em> &gt; <em>Relationships</em>: additional data to pull in and display, related in some way to the base data of the view (such as data about the user who created the content item).',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9320',
  'source' => '<em>Advanced</em> &gt; <em>Exposed form</em>: if you have exposed filters or sorts, how to display the form to the user.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9321',
  'source' => 'What are bulk operations?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9322',
  'source' => 'Views using a table display format can include a bulk operations form, which allows users with sufficient permission to select one or more items from the view and apply an administrative action to them. The bulk actions available are specific to the base data type of the view; for example, a view of content items could support bulk publishing and unpublishing actions. If you have the core Actions UI module installed, see the related topic "Configuring actions" for more about actions.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9323',
  'source' => 'Managing views overview',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9324',
  'source' => '<a href="https://www.drupal.org/docs/user_guide/en/views-chapter.html">Creating Listings with Views (Drupal User Guide)</a>',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9325',
  'source' => 'Create a new menu.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9326',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Add menu</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9327',
  'source' => 'Enter the title for the menu, which is used as the default block title if the menu is displayed as a block. If desired, also edit the machine name of the menu, which is by default derived from the title.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9328',
  'source' => 'Enter an administrative summary, which is displayed on the <em>Menus</em> page.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9329',
  'source' => 'If your site has more than one language, choose the language for the menu.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9330',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Save</em>. You will be on the menu editing page, ready to add links to the menu if the core Custom Menu Links module is installed; see related topics for further tasks.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9331',
  'source' => 'Locate the desired menu and click <em>Add link</em> in the <em>Operations</em> list.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9332',
  'source' => 'Enter the <em>Menu link title</em> to be displayed.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9333',
  'source' => 'Enter the <em>Link</em>, one of the following:',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9334',
  'source' => 'An internal path, such as <em>/node/add</em>',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9335',
  'source' => 'A full external URL',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9336',
  'source' => 'Start typing the title of a content item and select it when the full title comes up',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9337',
  'source' => '<em>&lt;nolink&gt;</em> to display the <em>Menu link title</em> as plain text without a link',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9338',
  'source' => '<em>&lt;front&gt;</em> to link to the front page of your site',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9339',
  'source' => 'Make sure that <em>Enabled</em> is checked; if not, the menu link will not be displayed.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9340',
  'source' => 'Optionally, enter a <em>Description</em>, which will be displayed when a user hovers over the link.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9341',
  'source' => 'Optionally, check <em>Show as expanded</em> to automatically show the children of this link (if any) when this link is shown.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9342',
  'source' => 'Optionally, select the <em>Parent link</em>, if this menu link should be a child of another menu link.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9343',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Save</em>. You will be returned to the <em>Add link</em> page to add another link.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9344',
  'source' => 'Locate the menu you just added a link to and click <em>Edit</em> in the <em>Operations</em> list.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9345',
  'source' => 'Verify that the order of links is correct. If it is not, drag menu links until the order is correct, and click <em>Save</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9346',
  'source' => 'For an existing content type, configure the available menus that will be shown as options on content editing screens; links to content items of this type can be added to these menus during editing.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9347',
  'source' => 'Under <em>Menu settings</em>, check the menus that you want to be available when editing a content item of this type.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9348',
  'source' => 'Optionally, select the <em>Default parent item</em>, to put links to content items under a default location in the menu structure.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9349',
  'source' => 'Disable menu links or change the order and hierarchy of menu links.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9350',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Edit menu</em> for the menu that you want to edit.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9351',
  'source' => 'Drag menu links into a new order, or check/uncheck <em>Enabled</em> to enable or disable menu links.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9352',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Save</em> to save your changes.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9353',
  'source' => 'Locate and open the content edit form for the content item, or create a new content item (see related topics on creating and editing content).',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9354',
  'source' => 'Under <em>URL alias</em> on the edit form, enter the desired alias (for example, "/about-us"). Make sure the alias starts with a "/" character.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9355',
  'source' => 'Who can manage URL aliases?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9356',
  'source' => 'Users with the <em>@path_permissions_link</em> permission can edit aliases.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9357',
  'source' => "In the <em>Manage</em> administration menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Search and metadata</em> &gt; <em>@path_aliases_link</em>. A list of all the site's aliases will appear.",
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9358',
  'source' => 'Make the required changes and click <em>Save</em>. You will be returned to the URL alias list page.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9359',
  'source' => "URL is the abbreviation for \"Uniform Resource Locator\", which is the page's address on the web. It is the \"name\" by which a browser identifies a page to display. In the example \"Visit us at <em>example.com</em>.\", <em>https://example.com</em> would be the URL for the home page of your website. Users use URLs to locate content on the web.",
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9360',
  'source' => 'What is a path?',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9361',
  'source' => 'A path is the unique, last part of the URL for a specific function or piece of content. For example, for a page whose full URL is <em>https://example.com/node/7</em>, the path is <em>node/7</em>. Here are some examples of paths you might find in your site:',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9362',
  'source' => 'user/3: Path to a user profile page.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9363',
  'source' => 'The core software allows you to provide more understandable URLs for pages on your site, which are called <em>aliases</em>. For example, if you have an "About Us" page with the path <em>node/7</em>, you can set up an alias of <em>about</em> so that your visitors will see it as <em>https://www.example.com/about</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9364',
  'source' => 'The core Path module provides the URL aliasing functionality. The contributed <a href="https://www.drupal.org/project/pathauto">Pathauto</a> module allows you to configure automatically-generated URL aliases for content items and other pages. See the related topics listed below for specific tasks.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9365',
  'source' => 'Concept: Paths, Aliases, and URLs (Drupal User Guide)',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9366',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Add responsive image style</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9367',
  'source' => 'Enter a descriptive <em>Label</em> for your style.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9368',
  'source' => 'Select a <em>Breakpoint group</em> from the groups defined by your installed themes and modules.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9369',
  'source' => 'On the next page, locate the fieldsets for the breakpoints provided by the selected <em>Breakpoint group</em>.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9370',
  'source' => 'For each breakpoint that you want to use, expand the corresponding fieldset. Select the <em>Select a single image style.</em> radio button under <em>Type</em> for the breakpoint, and select the <em>Image style</em> to use for images when that breakpoint is in effect. Repeat this step for the rest of the breakpoints you want to use.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9371',
  'source' => 'You can now use this responsive image style to format a field containing an image, in your layouts or traditional field displays. See related topics below for more information.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9372',
  'source' => 'Make sure that the core Shortcut module is installed, and that you have a role with <em>Edit current shortcut set</em> or <em>Administer shortcuts</em> permission. Also, make sure that a toolbar module is installed (either the core Toolbar module or a contributed module replacement).',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9373',
  'source' => 'Click <em>Shortcuts</em> in the toolbar to display your shortcuts, and verify that the list is complete.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9374',
  'source' => 'Optionally, click <em>Edit shortcuts</em> at the right end of the shortcut list (left end in right-to-left languages), to remove links or change their order.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9375',
  'source' => 'Users with the <em>@taxonomy_permissions_link</em> permission can configure a vocabulary. To add a field in the administrative interface, the core Field UI module must be installed, and you will also need the <em>Administer fields</em> permission for the entity you are adding the field to.',
  'context' => '',
  'version' => '11.0-dev',
])
->values([
  'lid' => '9376',
  'source' => 'Optionally, click <em>Add term</em> to add a term to the new vocabulary. In the <em>Name</em> field, enter the term name (for example "Butter"). Click <em>Save</em>. You will receive a confirmation about the term you created. You may optionally continue to add more terms.',
